//? Title: PixelRNN: A Causal Row-LSTM Image Density
//? Subtitle: Explicit row states, forget bias and a visible binary raster sampler
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A Row LSTM processes image rows sequentially while advancing every column in a
row together. A masked input convolution excludes the current and future raw
pixels; a1x3 convolution of the previous hidden row carries vertical context.
This compact native PixelRNN has357 stored values, four state channels and a
Bernoulli head on deterministic binary8x8 MNIST images. A worked memory example
precedes the real data. The complete program exposes all96 Adam writes and all64
raster-sampling steps. Exact causal checks, restored checkpoints and matching
source outputs establish numerical fidelity. The fixed-final test loss is.67764
bits per binary pixel, worse than the fit-only independent Bernoulli baseline's
.38445; the small run does not establish realistic digit generation.
)
\toc

> Recur over rows while predicting only from raster history

[van den Oord, Kalchbrenner and Kavukcuoglu, Pixel Recurrent Neural Networks
(2016)](https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.06759) models images through explicit pixel
dependencies. This source teaching variant uses one width4 Row-LSTM layer and a
binary head. It is not the paper's12-layer RGB architecture, a diagonal BiLSTM
or a classifier. There are no labels, pretrained weights, normalization,
pooling, dropout or batch-coupled statistics in the model.

A biased3x3 mask-A input convolution maps one gray channel to16 gate features.
At position(r,c), its raw receptive field includes the row above and the
current-left site, never the current or future pixel. The previous hidden row
is convolved with an unbiased1x3 kernel4→16. This may read neighboring columns
in the previous row; every such raw row is already in the raster past. Each
current row's columns advance in parallel, with no same-row hidden recurrence.
After all eight rows, a biased1x1 hidden4→1 head emits Bernoulli logits.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
