\caption(One row's computation. The next row receives both new state tensors. A1x3 kernel reads only the previous hidden row; the cell state has a separate forget/input update. The1x1 binary head is shared across all rows.)
\alttext(A previous hidden row enters an unbiased1x3 convolution, adds masked input gate features, and forms four gates. A previous cell row enters the cell update separately; the new hidden row produces binary logits.)

> Derive one step and hand-check the memory path

Let F_r be the masked input projection and W_row the recurrent kernel. Split
A_r=F_r+Conv(h_(r−1),W_row) into four contiguous width4 groups:

$$ i_r=\sigma(A_r^{(0)}),\quad f_r=\sigma(A_r^{(1)}+1),\quad
 o_r=\sigma(A_r^{(2)}),\quad g_r=\tanh(A_r^{(3)}), $$
$$ c_r=f_r\odot c_{r-1}+i_r\odot g_r,\qquad
 h_r=o_r\odot\tanh(c_r). $$
\alttext(Input, forget and output gates use sigmoid, the candidate uses tanh, and the forget group has a fixed plus-one offset. Cell combines forgotten old state and gated candidate; hidden gates the tanh cell.)

The ordering is **input, forget, output, candidate**. The fixed+1 forget offset
is not a learned recurrent bias. If both projections and W_row are zero,
i=o=.5, g=0 and f=sigmoid(1)=.73105858. Starting from an explicitly supplied
c_0=2 gives c_1=1.46211716 and h_1=.5*tanh(c_1). After k such steps,
c_k=2*f^k and its derivative with respect to c_0 is f^k. This exact toy fixes
the gates; the fitted model also has derivatives through its recurrent gates.

The real model starts from zero, using the source's **first input row features
times zero** for both h and c. This retains a connected, exactly zero input
path, including signed-zero arithmetic. It creates no learned initial state.
The nonzero toy state below is supplied solely to make forgetting visible.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_memory_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;auto t=n::DType::Float64;
 auto initial=n::full({1,1,1,4},2,t).requires_grad_();
 auto h=n::zeros({1,1,1,4},t),c=initial;
 const auto input=n::zeros({1,1,1,16},t),kernel=n::zeros({1,3,4,16},t);
 std::vector<double> rows{0},cells{2},hidden{0};
 for(int row=1;row<=8;++row){
  auto out=n::models::PixelRowLstmStep(input,h,c,kernel);h=out.hidden;c=out.cell;
  rows.push_back(row);cells.push_back(n::mean(c).item());hidden.push_back(n::mean(h).item());
 }
 auto partial=n::grad(n::sum(c),{initial})[0];
 auto f=n::sigmoid(n::ones({1},t));auto expected=n::pow(f,8);
 if(std::abs(partial.to_vector<double>()[0]-expected.item())>1e-14)throw std::runtime_error("Forget memory partial");
 auto loss=n::models::BernoulliPixels(n::zeros({1,8,8,1},t),n::ones({1,8,8,1},t)).loss;
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 plot.lines(rows,cells,"Cell memory",{37,99,235}).lines(rows,hidden,"Hidden output",{220,38,38})
 .y_limits(-.05,2.65).x_label("Explicit toy row transition").y_label("State value")
 .title("A fixed forget gate retains a geometric fraction of memory").save_svg("build/pr_memory_toy.svg");
 std::print("Forget gate={:.8f}; cell after8={:.8f}; initial-cell partial={:.8f}.\nZero-logit binary NLL={:.8f} nats/pixel, or1 bit.\n",f.item(),cells.back(),partial.to_vector<double>()[0],loss.item());
}
```
