> Inspect the exact input domain and the fixed-final density result

The top row below contains eight actual fit-pool gray8x8 images and the bottom
row their deterministic binary thresholds. The saved data archive also retains
all original28x28 images, labels and source IDs. Digit labels are metadata only;
PixelRNN does not use them for fitting or sampling.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_data.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;std::map<std::string,n::Tensor> a;
 for(auto& [key,value]:n::load_npz("build/pixel_rnn/data.npz"))a.emplace(key,value);
 auto rows=std::vector<std::int64_t>{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7};
 auto images=n::concat({n::take_rows(a.at("fit_gray"),rows),n::take_rows(a.at("fit_images"),rows)},0);
 n::save_image_grid(images,"build/pr_data.png",8,0,1,1,.5,8);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/pr_data.png)
// result_end

\caption(Actual gray inputs after8x8 interpolation, then their exact thresholded targets. A gray value exactly.5 becomes binary one. This deterministic binary domain defines every density score in the report.)
\alttext(Eight resized gray digit images appear above their black-and-white8x8 binary targets.)

The fit-only independent Bernoulli baseline has one parameter per site,
q_j=(count_j+.5)/1001. It evaluates test NLL with its unrounded Double posterior;
its saved probability image is separately Float32. It therefore preserves the
source scalar protocol rather than recomputing from rounded archived probabilities.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_history.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;std::map<std::string,n::Tensor> a;
 for(auto& [key,value]:n::load_npz("build/pixel_rnn/summary.npz"))a.emplace(key,value);
 double baseline=a.at("baseline_bits").item(),test=a.at("test_bits").item();
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 plot.lines({1,2,3},a.at("validation_bits").to_vector<double>(),"Validation",{37,99,235})
 .lines({1,3},{baseline,baseline},"Fit-only baseline",{22,163,74})
 .points({3},{test},"Fixed-final test",{220,38,38},6)
 .y_limits(.32,1.07).x_label("Fixed epoch").y_label("Bits per binary8x8 pixel")
 .title("The three-epoch Row LSTM still loses to its simple baseline").save_svg("build/pr_history.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/pr_history.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Validation falls from.90390 to.65999 bits per binary pixel. The fixed epoch3 test score is.67764, above the baseline's.38445. Lower is better. The report preserves this underfit result without adding epochs or selecting a different checkpoint.)
\alttext(Blue validation loss decreases across epochs1..3. The red final-test point remains above the horizontal green independent Bernoulli baseline.)

> Observe the row states and verify every causal restriction

The next figure summarizes the fitted model's actual hidden and cell sequences
on all200 test images. The hidden state is gated tanh(cell), while cell is an
accumulated recurrent state with a different scale. These are state magnitudes,
not predicted class probabilities, attribution scores or a proof of long-range
memory. Unlike the worked zero-kernel toy, the fitted gates depend on images
and earlier hidden rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_states.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;std::map<std::string,n::Tensor> a;
 for(auto& [key,value]:n::load_npz("build/pixel_rnn/outputs.npz"))a.emplace(key,value);
 auto hidden=n::mean(n::abs(n::cast(a.at("hidden"),n::DType::Float64)),{0,2,3});
 auto cell=n::mean(n::abs(n::cast(a.at("cell"),n::DType::Float64)),{0,2,3});
 const double high=1.45*n::max(n::concat({hidden,cell},0)).item();
 auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
 plot.lines(n::arange(0,8).to_vector<double>(),hidden.to_vector<double>(),"Hidden",{37,99,235})
 .lines(n::arange(0,8).to_vector<double>(),cell.to_vector<double>(),"Cell",{220,38,38})
 .y_limits(0,high).x_label("Image row, top0 to bottom7").y_label("Mean absolute state over test pool")
 .title("Gated hidden output and recurrent cell have different scales").save_svg("build/pr_states.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/pr_states.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Per-row mean absolute hidden and cell values, using all test images, columns and four state channels. These curves summarize actual saved state tensors after the fixed final fit.)
\alttext(Two curves show mean absolute hidden and cell state by row; cell magnitudes exceed their gated hidden outputs.)

For every logit j, the program checks all64 raw input derivatives: every
current/future entry k>=j is exactly zero. A separate finite-change guard flips
all current/future bits and requires every conditional through j to remain bit
identical. Thus64 whole-prefix guards complement4096 derivative entries.
Single-image inference also matches that image inside the full test batch.

The compact Row LSTM does not necessarily reach every past pixel. Current-row
information enters through the input mask's immediate-left site; vertical context
spreads through previous-row convolutions. Zeros below the causal diagonal can
reflect this finite field or fitted gate/weight values. Strict causality is the
required exclusion, not a claim that every earlier input is used.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pr_causality.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;n::Tensor j;
 for(auto& [key,value]:n::load_npz("build/pixel_rnn/causality.npz"))if(key=="jacobian")j=n::abs(value);
 auto scaled=n::log1p(100*j);scaled=scaled/n::max(scaled);
 n::save_image_grid(n::reshape(scaled,{1,64,64,1}),"build/pr_causality.png",1,0,1,0,0,6);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/pr_causality.png)
// result_end

\caption(Rows are output logits0..63 and columns raw input sites0..63. Display brightness is normalized log(1+100|derivative|). The entire current/future upper triangle, including the diagonal, is exactly zero in the saved unscaled Jacobian.)
\alttext(A64x64 derivative matrix has activity only below its diagonal; the current/future triangle is black.)

> Show all seeded samples and preserve observed prefixes

Every final sample begins from an all-zero canvas. The32 fixed-seed images
below are all source samples, without filtering. Checkpoint restoration preserves
all five leaves exactly; repeating the sampler reproduces images, uniform banks
and retained conditionals bit for bit. All inference leaves fitted weights unchanged.

